## Introduction: Why Most Supabase RLS Implementations Leak Data

When building full-stack applications with Next.js 14 and Supabase, developers frequently misunderstand the relationship between server-side data fetching and database policies. By default, querying Supabase using the `service_role` key completely bypasses Row Level Security (RLS). If an API route or Server Action accidentally executes with service privileges instead of user context, private user data is exposed.

In this production guide, we cover the exact architectural patterns required to implement bulletproof PostgreSQL RLS policies that execute in under 15ms while maintaining full SSR performance in Next.js.

---

## 1. Setting Up Database Policies for Multi-Tenant Isolation

To ensure users can only read and write their own records, every table should include a `user_id` column referencing `auth.users(id)` with a foreign key constraint.

```sql
-- Create projects table with strict user reference
CREATE TABLE public.user_projects (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES auth.users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  title TEXT NOT NULL,
  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active',
  metadata JSONB DEFAULT '{}'::jsonb,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

-- 1. Enable Row Level Security immediately
ALTER TABLE public.user_projects ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

-- 2. CREATE POLICY: User can only select their own records
CREATE POLICY "Users can read own projects"
ON public.user_projects
FOR SELECT
TO authenticated
USING (auth.uid() = user_id);

-- 3. CREATE POLICY: User can only insert records tagged with their own UID
CREATE POLICY "Users can insert own projects"
ON public.user_projects
FOR INSERT
TO authenticated
WITH CHECK (auth.uid() = user_id);

-- 4. CREATE POLICY: User can only update their own records
CREATE POLICY "Users can update own projects"
ON public.user_projects
FOR UPDATE
TO authenticated
USING (auth.uid() = user_id)
WITH CHECK (auth.uid() = user_id);

-- 5. CREATE POLICY: User can only delete their own records
CREATE POLICY "Users can delete own projects"
ON public.user_projects
FOR DELETE
TO authenticated
USING (auth.uid() = user_id);
```

### Critical Performance Index
Always add a B-Tree index on the `user_id` column. Without this, every RLS query performs a full table scan across all tenants:

```sql
CREATE INDEX idx_user_projects_user_id ON public.user_projects(user_id);
```

---

## 2. Server-Side Supabase Client in Next.js 14 App Router

In Next.js 14 App Router, create a server client that automatically passes the user's session cookies down to Supabase so `auth.uid()` evaluates accurately:

```typescript
// lib/supabase/server.ts
import { createServerClient, type CookieOptions } from '@supabase/ssr';
import { cookies } from 'next/headers';

export function createClient() {
  const cookieStore = cookies();

  return createServerClient(
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL!,
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY!,
    {
      cookies: {
        get(name: string) {
          return cookieStore.get(name)?.value;
        },
        set(name: string, value: string, options: CookieOptions) {
          try {
            cookieStore.set({ name, value, ...options });
          } catch (error) {
            // The `set` method was called from a Server Component.
          }
        },
        remove(name: string, options: CookieOptions) {
          try {
            cookieStore.set({ name, value: '', ...options });
          } catch (error) {
            // The `delete` method was called from a Server Component.
          }
        },
      },
    }
  );
}
```

---

## 3. Querying with RLS in Server Components & Server Actions

Now, any query executed in a React Server Component automatically respects the user's RLS boundaries without manual `WHERE user_id = ...` filtering:

```typescript
// app/dashboard/page.tsx
import { createClient } from '@/lib/supabase/server';
import { redirect } from 'next/navigation';

export default async function DashboardPage() {
  const supabase = createClient();

  // Check authentication
  const { data: { user }, error: authError } = await supabase.auth.getUser();
  if (authError || !user) {
    redirect('/login');
  }

  // RLS automatically filters results to auth.uid() = user.id
  const { data: projects, error } = await supabase
    .from('user_projects')
    .select('id, title, status, created_at')
    .order('created_at', { ascending: false });

  if (error) {
    console.error('Failed to load projects:', error.message);
    return <div className="text-red-500">Error loading projects.</div>;
  }

  return (
    <main className="p-8 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <h1 className="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Your Projects ({projects.length})</h1>
      <ul className="divide-y divide-slate-800">
        {projects.map((p) => (
          <li key={p.id} className="py-3 flex justify-between items-center">
            <span className="font-medium">{p.title}</span>
            <span className="text-xs px-2 py-1 bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 rounded-full">
              {p.status}
            </span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </main>
  );
}
```

---

## 4. Key Takeaways & Security Checklist

1. **Never use the `service_role` key** on public endpoints or client components.
2. **Always test RLS policies** with unauthenticated requests to verify that 0 rows are returned.
3. **Index all foreign keys and policy filter columns** (`user_id`, `org_id`, `role`).
4. **Enforce `security_invoker = true`** on PostgreSQL views so that views inherit underlying table RLS.

